[
  {
    "pkheadingid": 821,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Definition",
    "headingPosition": 1,
    "headingid": 764,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128260,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 764,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Dermal hyperneury represents a form of small nerve hypertrophy characterized by unusually large and prominent dermal nerve fibres. Epithelial sheath neuroma has similar histological features, with the added finding of mature squamous epithelium surrounding the perineurium.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 822,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-O coding",
    "headingPosition": 2,
    "headingid": 765,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128261,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 765,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>9563/0 Dermal hyperneury&nbsp;/ epithelial sheath neuroma</p>\r\n<p>9563/0 Syndromic dermal hyperneury / epithelial sheath neuroma</p>\r\n<p>9563/0 Sporadic dermal hyperneury / epithelial sheath neuroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 823,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "ICD-11 coding",
    "headingPosition": 3,
    "headingid": 766,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128262,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 766,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>2F24 &amp; XH4UE6 Benign cutaneous neoplasms of neural or nerve sheath origin &amp; Neuroma, NOS</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 824,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Related terminology",
    "headingPosition": 4,
    "headingid": 767,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128263,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 767,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Not recommended:</em> multiple cutaneous neuromas.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 825,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Subtype(s)",
    "headingPosition": 5,
    "headingid": 768,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128264,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 768,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Syndromic dermal hyperneury&nbsp;/ epithelial sheath neuroma; sporadic dermal hyperneury&nbsp;/ epithelial sheath neuroma</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 826,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Localization",
    "headingPosition": 6,
    "headingid": 769,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128265,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 769,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Periorificial and mucosal lesions (lips, eyelid, tongue) appear in multiple endocrine neoplasia type&nbsp;2B (MEN2B) {30660595}. Cutaneous nerves are enlarged in patients with MEN2B, even in the absence of visible lesions {6127365}. Outside a syndromic setting, there is a slight predilection for the trunk {31216366}. Epithelial sheath neuroma shows a predilection for the back {10680886}.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 827,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Clinical features",
    "headingPosition": 7,
    "headingid": 770,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128266,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 770,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The lesions are single or multiple and asymptomatic. Rarely, patients may experience itching, intermittent burning, or pain.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 828,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Epidemiology",
    "headingPosition": 8,
    "headingid": 771,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128267,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 771,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Mucocutaneous neural proliferations have been described in syndromic patients as young as 5&nbsp;years old {16702501}. Most of the patients in the sporadic setting are in their seventh decade of life. There is a slight female preponderance.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 829,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Etiology",
    "headingPosition": 9,
    "headingid": 772,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128268,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 772,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The etiology is unknown. In terms of genetic factors, mucocutaneous neural proliferations have been described in MEN2B {6127365}, multiple endocrine neoplasia type 2A {29049491}, neurofibromatosis type&nbsp;2 {18715250}, and Cowden disease {3698331}. Epithelial sheath neuroma is only sporadic.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 830,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Pathogenesis",
    "headingPosition": 10,
    "headingid": 773,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128269,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 773,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not entirely elucidated but may involve PTEN-regulated and RET-regulated signalling pathways {26604880}, particularly in syndromic cases.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 837,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Macroscopic appearance",
    "headingPosition": 11,
    "headingid": 780,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128270,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 780,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>In MEN2B syndrome, enlarged nerve fibres may look like normal skin. When visible, lesions are relatively small, pink or skin-coloured papules and nodules.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 831,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Histopathology",
    "headingPosition": 12,
    "headingid": 774,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128271,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 774,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Increase in nerve fibre&ndash;aggregated neural tissue is observed within the dermis or the dermal/subcutaneous interface. Histological changes are identical in syndromic and non-syndromic cases. Nerve fibres are 1.5&ndash;4&nbsp;times as large as normal nerves. Mild neuroma-like internal nerve fibre disorganization can be seen. Lymphoplasmacytic and histiocytic perineurial inflammation or intraneural mucin accumulation may be seen. In epithelial sheath neuroma, enlarged nerve fibres are ensheathed by squamous epithelium. Subcutaneous extension is exceptional {29797343}. Occasionally, dermal hyperneury presents incidentally to other lesions such as actinic keratosis, basal cell carcinoma, and nodular prurigo.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 832,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Cytology",
    "headingPosition": 13,
    "headingid": 775,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128272,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 775,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 833,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Diagnostic molecular pathology",
    "headingPosition": 14,
    "headingid": 776,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128273,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 776,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Multiple lesions in younger patients should prompt investigation for genetic syndromes and pertinent molecular alterations.</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 836,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Essential and desirable diagnostic criteria",
    "headingPosition": 15,
    "headingid": 779,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128274,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 779,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p><em>Essential:</em> enlarged and prominent dermal nerve fibres; nerve sheath squamous cells in epithelial sheath neuroma.</p>\r\n<p><em>Desirable:</em> mild neuroma-like internal disorganization (in some cases).</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 834,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Staging",
    "headingPosition": 16,
    "headingid": 777,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128275,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 777,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>Not clinically relevant</p>\r\n",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  },
  {
    "pkheadingid": 835,
    "bookid": 64,
    "headingType": 2,
    "headingTitle": "Prognosis and prediction",
    "headingPosition": 17,
    "headingid": 778,
    "contributions": [
      {
        "pkcontributionId": 128276,
        "bookId": 64,
        "chapterId": 312,
        "headingId": 778,
        "headingText": "\r\n<p>The clinical course is benign.</p>",
        "timestamp": "2025-04-25T14:48:37"
      }
    ],
    "booknotes": [],
    "numberofBooknote": 0
  }
]